demy-id "q-320671" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{2}{3}M+2N$, where\n$M=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 15 \\\\ -12 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$N=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{2} & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $\\frac{2}{3}M+2N=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-2$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-12$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $M$ by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and each entry of $N$ by $2,$ and then add the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}M+2N & =\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 15 \\\\ -12 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{2} & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 6 & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 15 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-12) & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 6 & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-1) & 2\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-2) & 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\cdot (-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4+6 & 0-2 & 10+0 \\\\ -8-4 & 4+1 & 0-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -2 & 10 \\\\ -12 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43f31230-0191-5a33-9bb6-0fa7b614f351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 15 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 14 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392961} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcc9309-5767-5a6f-9aa6-d329adc4d01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 11 & 14\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392962} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf8254f-ba83-5a63-900a-3e1c1e498e5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 14 \\\\ 2 & 11 & 15\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392963} {:answer/id #uuid "8d6b4403-a5b1-58bb-a750-3f579f43ba12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 11 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392964} {:answer/id #uuid "fb62ffb5-df7e-583b-a72f-d8637500006d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & 15 \\\\ -2 & 11 & 14\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbcc9309-5767-5a6f-9aa6-d329adc4d01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 11 & 14\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392962} :answer-field/id #uuid "18f4324f-d0d0-557c-90a1-7c9edabe233c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392960}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a24360c-add3-5cc0-aec3-f47a46ccf0fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-69642" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{1}{3}G+2H$, where\n$G=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 15 \\\\ -12 & 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$H=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $G$ by $\\frac{1}{3}$ and each entry of $H$ by $2,$ and then add the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}G+2H & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 15 \\\\ -12 & 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3 & \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6 & \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 15 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-12) & \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9 & \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 & 2\\cdot 5 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot 4 & 2\\cdot 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+2 & 2+4 & 5+10 \\\\ -4+6 & 3+8 & 2+12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 11 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Linear Combination of Two Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110754 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110456 :question/id #uuid "42313322-7535-5969-ad84-958449e48c1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3480" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\dfrac{5}{9}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1 .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\dfrac{5}{9}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t        x^2+\\left(\\dfrac{5}{9}\\right)^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2+\\left(\\dfrac{25}{81}\\right)&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2&= 1-\\dfrac{25}{81}\\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2&= \\dfrac{56}{81}\\[5pt]\n\t\tx&= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{56}{81}}\\[5pt]\n\t        x&= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.\n}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive. So, we select the positive answer. \n\nTherefore, our final answer is $x=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049224 :topic/math-academy-id 433 :topic/title "The Pythagorean Theorem"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e88fcef-2ef9-533c-a8ca-bcedb24c445f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107636} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c3d912-edb9-5612-a883-39811dac0b62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107637} {:answer/id #uuid "cea0e368-fdbb-5547-b6cd-900a17d25537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{\\sqrt{31}}{8}"