uting $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{2}{9}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c11b11b-3274-5c34-b09c-f5ec78de4e43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257177} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcebdf8-f732-50f7-b15a-29792fc1f881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257450} {:answer/id #uuid "52f6bed6-51b4-5841-9d86-c1222468a4fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257491} {:answer/id #uuid "5d59303b-e2ee-5904-a3d2-9f17c7fcfaa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257597} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb144b3-687e-5d43-94a0-00b76d2854a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52f6bed6-51b4-5841-9d86-c1222468a4fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257491} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd602cdd-cbaf-5de8-8895-9b474285c26d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259393}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66205715-ba82-541e-8ac0-31ae0009bc90" :question/math-academy-id "q-124611" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{5}{9}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e2c5600-797d-5dbf-82c6-38d1f84faa2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257030} {:answer/id #uuid "74fd787b-5a48-5721-ace4-53d2a3ae18c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257805} {:answer/id #uuid "8eaeb7d8-bab9-59df-bc4b-9bc4a2cd6abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258039} {:answer/id #uuid "df96c081-601c-58bb-a7e3-d920b7e1b2fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258730} {:answer/id #uuid "f62bb330-cd3c-5d2d-bc3f-9d00a76f8b7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df96c081-601c-58bb-a7e3-d920b7e1b2fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258730} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae52c65d-229d-5240-a6b7-89e661e7fe0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259337}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19802aad-5d1c-554b-bf69-ef21c3999563" :question/math-academy-id "q-124613" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.$ What is the $y$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}$ into the above and solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{16}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{11}{16} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{16}} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a71e23-0acc-56b3-954d-83821eac7e61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288050} {:answer/id #uuid "44b4646f-0c19-57a3-bdf3-8ad3609e015a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288498} {:answer/id #uuid "7769db55-4f82-5ad4-abd8-f7f093ba576d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288856} {:answer/id #uuid "bd830b0e-ef09-5fa8-8c16-55c6bc0ac78d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289280} {:answer/id #uuid "dc81901a-30cb-5088-a113-f77b69120c2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd830b0e-ef09-